    "background": "The Committee for Orphan Medicinal Products (COMP) is the European Medicines Agency's (EMA) committee responsible for recommending orphan designation of medicines for rare diseases. It was established in 2000 when new legislation was introduced to encourage the development of medicinal products for rare diseases. One important criterion for granting an orphan designation to a medicine is a prevalence threshold of maximum 5 in 10,000 for the associated condition at the time of application. Of the submissions to the COMP, around 35% are for oncological conditions and around 42% of designations of orphan medicinal products in oncology granted by the COMP between 2000 and 2015 related to rare haematological malignancies. 1",
    "aim": "What is the prevalence of rare blood cancers in Europe?",
    "objectives": [
      {
        "id": "obj_1",
        "text": "To estimate the prevalence of follicular lymphoma between 1 st January 2010 and the end of available data in data sources from across Europe, stratified by age and sex"
      },
      {
        "id": "obj_2",
        "text": "To estimate the prevalence of diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma between 1 st January 2010 and the end of available data in data sources from across Europe, stratified by age and sex"
      },
      {
        "id": "obj_3",
        "text": "To estimate the prevalence of multiple myeloma between 1 st January 2010 and the end of available data in data sources from across Europe, stratified by age and sex"
      },
      {
        "id": "obj_4",
        "text": "To estimate the prevalence of chronic lymphocytic leukaemia between 1 st January 2010 and the end of available data in data sources from across Europe, stratified by age and sex"
      },
      {
        "id": "obj_5",
        "text": "To estimate the prevalence of acute myeloid leukaemia between 1 st January 2010 and the end of available data in data sources from across Europe, stratified by age and sex"
      },
      {
        "id": "obj_6",
        "text": "To estimate the prevalence of acute lymphocytic leukaemia between 1 st January 2010 and the end of available data in data sources from across Europe, stratified by age and sex Table 1. Primary research questions and objectives"
      }
    ],
